      "writtenOn": "2023-07-14T20:26:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I like the concept but I think we could be more explicit here. We only have three options, `EC_RES_BUSY`, `EC_RES_SUCCESS`, or `EC_RES_ERROR`. Having blocks for each of those three (although I used a `!\u003d EC_RES_SUCCESS` for `EC_RES_BUSY` to handle weird error cases) will make it very obvious what\u0027s going on. \n\n```\nif (rc \u003d\u003d EC_RES_BUSY)\n    return EC_RES_BUSY;\nelse if (rc !\u003d EC_RES_SUCCESS) {\n   /* Assuming an error, clear it and wait for the next request. */\n   flash_protect_async_data.rc \u003d EC_RES_SUCCESS;\n   return EC_RES_ERROR;\n} else {\n   /* rc is EC_RES_SUCCESS */\n   r-\u003eflags \u003d crec_flash_get_protect();\n\n   /* Indicate which flags are valid on this platform */\n   r-\u003evalid_flags \u003d EC_FLASH_PROTECT_GPIO_ASSERTED |\n                 EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ERROR_STUCK |\n                 EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ERROR_INCONSISTENT |\n                 EC_FLASH_PROTECT_ERROR_UNKNOWN |\n                 crec_flash_physical_get_valid_flags();\n   r-\u003ewritable_flags \u003d\n                 crec_flash_physical_get_writable_flags(r-\u003eflags);\n\n   args-\u003eresponse_size \u003d sizeof(*r);\n   return EC_RES_SUCCESS;\n} \n```",
